    <paragraph>65, 66, 67, and 68 Improper Integrals that Are Both Type 1 and Type 2 The integral $$\int_{a}^{\infty}f(x)dx$$ is improper because the interval $$\lbrack a,\infty)$$ is infinite. If $$f$$ has an infinite discontinuity at $$a$$ , then the integral is improper for a second reason. In this case we evaluate the integral by expressing it as a sum of improper integrals of Type 2 and Type 1 as follows:</paragraph>
    <paragraph math="block">$$\int_{a}^{\infty}f(x)dx = \int_{a}^{c}f(x)dx + \int_{c}^{\infty}f(x)dxc &gt; a$$</paragraph>
    <paragraph>Evaluate the given integral if it is convergent.</paragraph>
