    <paragraph>85, 86, and 87 The Laplace Transform If $$f(t)$$ is continuous for $$t \geq 0$$ , the Laplace transform of $$f$$ is the function $$F$$ defined by</paragraph>
    <paragraph math="block">$$F(s) = {\int_{0}^{\infty}{f(t)e^{- st}}}dt$$</paragraph>
    <paragraph>and the domain of $$F$$ is the set consisting of all numbers $$s$$ for which the integral converges.</paragraph>
    <paragraph math="block">$$F(s) = {\int_{0}^{\infty}{f(t)e^{- st}}}dt$$</paragraph>
    <exercise number="85" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find the Laplace transform of each of the following functions.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>$$f(t) = 1$$</item>
          <item>$$f(t) = e^{ t}$$</item>
          <item>$$f(t) = t$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$F(s) = 1 \slash s$$ , $$s &gt; 0$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$F(s) = 1 \slash \left( {s - 1} \right)$$ , $$s &gt; 1$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$F(s) = 1 \slash s^{2}$$ , $$s &gt; 0$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
